  "query_preview": "..The latest satellite imagery shows extensive cloud \ncover east of a Nogales to Tucson to Casa Grande line. The KEMX \nradar indicates stratiform precipitation across portions of Cochise \nCounty, although lightning strikes/thunderstorms have been noted \nalong and to the south of the International Border over the last \ncouple of hours. Precipitation amounts are generally light with \nthese showers, between a couple of hundredths up to 0.25 inches with \nthe heaviest showers. Over the last 6 hours, a few locations in the \nsouthern and eastern mountains of Cochise County, such as the \nHuachuca's, the Mule's and the Chiricahuca's have recorded between \n0.50 and 0.75 inches of rainfall.  \n\nOtherwise, southeast Arizona remains in southerly flow aloft between \na trough to the west and high pressure aloft centered over northeast \nMexico and Texas. PWATs across the forecast area range from around \n0.80 inches in the White Mountains of northern Greenlee County to \nnear 1.25 inches along the International Border. The current \nforecast has a chance of showers/thunderstorms from Sells eastward \ntoday. The high res models keep the shower activity going over the \neastern counties through the morning hours, then favor the western \nedge of the cloud shield this afternoon and evening for stronger \nthunderstorm development where insolation will result in steeper \nlapse rates. High temperatures today will top out 4 degrees below \nnormal from Tucson westward and 6-8 degrees below normal across the \neastern counties under the thicker cloud shield.   \n\nOverall, the forecast looks on track. No updates this morning to the \ngrids or text products. The main threat from the storms today will \nbe heavy rainfall, with a secondary threat of gusty outflows with \nthe strongest storms. For more information on the latter periods of \nthe forecast, please refer to the PREV DISCUSSION section below.",
